  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia works closely with national and sub-national authorities to promote inclusive service delivery in Cambodia. Prior to the ACCESS 2 program, marginalised communities were often excluded from local decision-making, leading to disparities in basic service access. ACCESS 2 aims to enhance capacity and promote collaboration between government bodies and civil society organisations, including Organisations for Persons with Disabilities (OPDs), to advocate for inclusive decision making. Technical support on Gender Equality, Disability and Social Inclusion (GEDSI) mainstreaming was provided to 65 OPDs and target service providers, which led to an increased number of representatives from marginalised groups participating in existing sub-national governance mechanisms. In 2024, 51 persons with disabilities (21 women) were appointed as members of Commune Committees for Women and Children across 51 communes. Additionally, 12 OPD representatives (eight women) were actively engaged in the four Provincial Disability Action Councils, advocating for the rights of persons with disabilities. Sub-national authorities also increased community consultations with Gender Based Violence (GBV) survivors, indigenous groups and persons with disabilities during workplan development.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia works closely with the Ministry of Health (MoH) to strengthen Cambodia&#x2019;s rehabilitation system. Through targeted technical assistance, the MoH has expanded service coverage, improved policy coherence, and increased formal recognition of the rehabilitation workforce. In 2024, supported public facilities delivered centre-based rehabilitation services to 15,348 persons with disabilities (PwDs) (25 per cent women). Community-based services reached an additional 6,272 PwDs. Four hospital-based Prosthetics and Orthotics (PO) Units were established; three in public hospitals and one in a private hospital. Additionally, three Physical Rehabilitation Centres distributed 4,742 assistive devices to clients (1,151 women). At the policy level, the MoH has demonstrated a strong commitment to integrating rehabilitation into the health system by developing legal frameworks to expand rehabilitation in public healthcare facilities. These include a draft guideline that sets minimum standards for hospital rehabilitation wards, a draft regulation that legally defines &#x201c;medical rehabilitation&#x201d; for public health facilities, and the National Health Workforce Development Plan 2024-2033, which formally recognises rehabilitation professionals. These regulatory improvements are expected to significantly increase access to rehabilitation services for PwDs across Cambodia&#x2019;s public health system.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia works with the Ministry of Women&#x2019;s Affairs and sub-national administrations to improve the quality and expand access to GBV services in Cambodia. Through technical assistance from ACCESS 2, provincial GBV Response Working Groups capacities have been strengthened, leading to more effective support for survivors and improved service delivery across four target provinces. In 2024, government and non-government actors delivered 1,153 GBV services to 906 survivors (including five women with disabilities). These included counselling (533), legal aid (315), medical care (69), social affairs support (210), and referrals (26). 
&#x200d;
Over 60 per cent of clients reported satisfaction with GBV service quality, based on initial rounds of delivery. As providers build experience and systems become more established, satisfaction rates are expected to increase. In addition, over 1,000 GBV-trained service providers (561 women, including 173 with disabilities) improved capacity to apply national Essential Service Guidelines. For example, one trainee from an Indigenous community in Ratanakiri province now leads awareness campaigns and collaborates with local authorities to safeguard survivors&#x2019; safety and confidentiality. Targeted sub-national authorities have also established 18 confidential counselling rooms at provincial, district and commune levels to provide safe spaces and counselling support for women and girls affected by GBV.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
